    "targets": [
      {
        "kind": "ui",
        "id": "tour-overlay"
      }
    ],
    "html": "<p>A tour is a card whose body holds steps. A step names what it is about, the camera it wants, a\ncaption, and what advances it; playing the tour flies the camera, spotlights the step&#39;s targets and\ndims everything else, with a scrubber and a way out.</p>\n<p>A tour is one document on three surfaces. The card&#39;s own inspector authors the steps, the overlay is\nthe reader&#39;s view with no inspector open, and the handbook can start a tour whose steps include the\narticle&#39;s subject.</p>\n<p>A step whose target no longer exists falls back to the camera it stored and says the thing is\nmissing, rather than silently skipping. That is the difference between a tour that has aged and a\ntour that is broken.</p>\n<p>Tours are paused in this build. The block, the schema and the overlay exist; the row that inserts a\nstep is not in the <code>/</code> menu while the feature is being reworked, and no part of the product\nadvertises it.</p>\n",
    "text": "A tour is a card whose body holds steps. A step names what it is about, the camera it wants, a caption, and what advances it; playing the tour flies the camera, spotlights the step's targets and dims everything else, with a scrubber and a way out. A tour is one document on three surfaces. The card's own inspector authors the steps, the overlay is the reader's view with no inspector open, and the handbook can start a tour whose steps include the article's subject. A step whose target no longer exists falls back to the camera it stored and says the thing is missing, rather than silently skipping. That is the difference between a tour that has aged and a tour that is broken. Tours are paused in this build. The block, the schema and the overlay exist; the row that inserts a step is not in the / menu while the feature is being reworked, and no part of the product advertises it."
