  {
    "id": "collaboration/saving",
    "slug": "saving",
    "title": "Saving and offline",
    "description": "Why there is no Save button, and what happens when the network is not there.",
    "section": "collaboration",
    "order": 20,
    "targets": [],
    "html": "<p>Every edit is a change in the workspace&#39;s log, written to this browser first and sent to the relay\nsecond. There is no Save button because there is no unsaved state to give it: closing the tab after\na keystroke keeps the keystroke, and the status pill reports the only thing worth reporting, whether\nthis device&#39;s copy is durable and whether the relay has caught up.</p>\n<p>Offline is a normal state rather than an error. The canvas keeps working, edits queue, and the pill\nsays how many are waiting. Reconnecting sends them; a conflict is resolved by the document&#39;s own\nmerge rules rather than by asking which version to keep.</p>\n<p>Two facts about the local copy matter and are said plainly in the sync panel: where the copy lives,\nand what the browser has promised to keep. A browser clears an origin&#39;s data without asking, so a\ncopy that is not durable is reported as such, with <em>Export a copy</em> as the one action that survives\na cleanup.</p>\n<p>The storage ladder warns before it is a problem: a neutral line past 70% and an urgent one past\n90%, each offering the same export.</p>\n",
    "text": "Every edit is a change in the workspace's log, written to this browser first and sent to the relay second. There is no Save button because there is no unsaved state to give it: closing the tab after a keystroke keeps the keystroke, and the status pill reports the only thing worth reporting, whether this device's copy is durable and whether the relay has caught up. Offline is a normal state rather than an error. The canvas keeps working, edits queue, and the pill says how many are waiting. Reconnecting sends them; a conflict is resolved by the document's own merge rules rather than by asking which version to keep. Two facts about the local copy matter and are said plainly in the sync panel: where the copy lives, and what the browser has promised to keep. A browser clears an origin's data without asking, so a copy that is not durable is reported as such, with Export a copy as the one action that survives a cleanup. The storage ladder warns before it is a problem: a neutral line past 70% and an urgent one past 90%, each offering the same export."
  },
  {
    "id": "account/plans",
    "slug": "plans",
    "title": "Plans",
    "description": "Why a workspace can be refused, and what a plan has to do with it.",
    "section": "account",
    "order": 20,
    "targets": [],
    "html": "<p>A workspace is either held by an account or it is local. A plan is what puts one in an account and\nkeeps it syncing; without one the relay refuses with a reason, and the sync panel names that reason\nrather than showing a spinner.</p>\n<p>The refusal is not a lock on your data. The local copy stays exactly where it is, the canvas keeps\nworking, and exporting is always available. What stops is the relay&#39;s willingness to hold the\nworkspace for an account that is not paying for it.</p>\n<p>The panel that says this also carries the door: <strong>See plans</strong> opens the account panel on its plan\ntab, which is the only place that can fix it. The launcher repeats the same notice beside the\nworkspaces this browser holds, because a reader who has just paid elsewhere needs the list to\nrefresh — so it is read again when the tab comes back and when the account panel closes.</p>\n",
    "text": "A workspace is either held by an account or it is local. A plan is what puts one in an account and keeps it syncing; without one the relay refuses with a reason, and the sync panel names that reason rather than showing a spinner. The refusal is not a lock on your data. The local copy stays exactly where it is, the canvas keeps working, and exporting is always available. What stops is the relay's willingness to hold the workspace for an account that is not paying for it. The panel that says this also carries the door: See plans opens the account panel on its plan tab, which is the only place that can fix it. The launcher repeats the same notice beside the workspaces this browser holds, because a reader who has just paid elsewhere needs the list to refresh — so it is read again when the tab comes back and when the account panel closes."
  },
  {
    "id": "account/signing-in",
    "slug": "signing-in",
    "title": "Signing in",
    "description": "What an account adds, and what a browser keeps without one.",
    "section": "account",
    "order": 10,
    "targets": [],
    "html": "<p>A workspace works without an account. It lives in this browser, syncs when a relay is reachable, and\nexports to a file. Signing in adds the things a browser cannot do: membership in an organization,\ninvitations, devices, and a licence.</p>\n<p>Signing in is not what makes a workspace collaborative. Sharing a board is a link; a relay is a\nserver. An account is what makes a workspace <em>yours</em> across machines, and what lets an operator\nanswer questions about it.</p>\n<p>An account can also be required, at which point an unsigned browser is admitted as local-only and\noffered a sign-in. The door is stated rather than assumed: nothing about the canvas changes shape\nwhen you sign in, and nothing is lost when you sign out.</p>\n<p>Devices and sessions are listed in the account panel, because &quot;where am I signed in&quot; is a question\npeople ask after a laptop goes missing.</p>\n",
    "text": "A workspace works without an account. It lives in this browser, syncs when a relay is reachable, and exports to a file. Signing in adds the things a browser cannot do: membership in an organization, invitations, devices, and a licence. Signing in is not what makes a workspace collaborative. Sharing a board is a link; a relay is a server. An account is what makes a workspace yours across machines, and what lets an operator answer questions about it. An account can also be required, at which point an unsigned browser is admitted as local-only and offered a sign-in. The door is stated rather than assumed: nothing about the canvas changes shape when you sign in, and nothing is lost when you sign out. Devices and sessions are listed in the account panel, because \"where am I signed in\" is a question people ask after a laptop goes missing."
  }
